     "updated": "2010-07-13T18:16:01.629-0300"
    },
    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "6089a4cb543264006867626e",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Stephen Bayliss",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a4cb543264006867626e",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": {
      "content": [
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Correction: FeSL is using XACML 2 (schema at least, but an \"interim\" build of the Sun engine post 1.0).",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "The issues are likely to be how the policy combining algorithms work.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "FeSL currently uses the hierarchical resource profile specified in XACML 2.0, and has hierarchical policy combining algorithms, but it's not known how (if) these will work with multiple hierarchies.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "The other \"standard\" combining algorithms should be fine.  However the hierarchical ones are probably the most useful (if we can figure out how they should work in this scenario, and then make them work).",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Currently we have (eg for hierarchical-deny-overrides",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "attrs": {
           "id": "1f642",
           "shortName": ":slight_smile:",
           "text": "🙂"
          },
          "type": "emoji"
         },
         {
          "type": "hardBreak"
         },
         {
          "text": "/coll1/coll2/res1:",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Policy at coll1 level is overriden by policy at coll2 level etc, with multiple policies on the same level treated as \"deny overrides\".",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "What should happen if we have",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "type": "hardBreak"
         },
         {
          "text": "/coll1/coll2/res1:",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "type": "hardBreak"
         },
         {
          "text": "/collA/res1:",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "with policies applied for all collections.  Does the \"level\" at which the policy applies even make any sense over two hierarchies?  It would seem that if we could combine policies independently down the two hierarchies, and then apply a simple \"deny overrides\" (eg) to combine the two resulting policies that would make sense?",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       }
      ],
      "type": "doc",
      "version": 1
     },
